1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,916
Every conversation on a network needs two things: an address, and a door number.

2
00:00:05,466 --> 00:00:09,882
The address finds the computer. The door number finds the program on that computer.

3
00:00:10,432 --> 00:00:13,228
Today you learn how those door numbers,

4
00:00:13,228 --> 00:00:14,159
called ports,

5
00:00:14,159 --> 00:00:14,518
work,

6
00:00:14,518 --> 00:00:17,600
and how TCP and UDP carry the conversation.

7
00:00:18,150 --> 00:00:22,417
You will watch ports open, connections form, and a port say no, all live.

8
00:00:24,467 --> 00:00:25,555
First, ports.

9
00:00:26,105 --> 00:00:30,030
A computer runs many programs that use the network at the same time.

10
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:35,082
The IP address finds the computer. The port number finds the right program.

11
00:00:35,632 --> 00:00:40,709
A port is just a number, from zero to sixty-five thousand five hundred thirty-five.

12
00:00:41,259 --> 00:00:42,803
Some are well known.

13
00:00:42,803 --> 00:00:44,193
Twenty-two is SSH.

14
00:00:44,193 --> 00:00:45,659
Fifty-three is DNS.

15
00:00:45,659 --> 00:00:47,358
Eighty is web traffic.

16
00:00:47,358 --> 00:00:50,368
Four forty-three is secure web traffic.

17
00:00:50,668 --> 00:00:55,319
Numbers up to one thousand twenty-three are reserved for well-known services.

18
00:00:55,869 --> 00:00:57,384
When your browser connects,

19
00:00:57,384 --> 00:00:59,573
it picks a random high port for itself,

20
00:00:59,573 --> 00:01:02,098
and connects to the server's well-known port.

21
00:01:04,148 --> 00:01:10,024
Your computer keeps a small list of well-known names and numbers.

22
00:01:10,024 --> 00:01:13,279
Type: getent services ssh http https

23
00:01:13,409 --> 00:01:18,422
Each line shows a name, a port number, and the protocol. All three use TCP.

24
00:01:18,972 --> 00:01:22,741
Now let us see a real program listening.

25
00:01:22,741 --> 00:01:26,604
We started a small web server beforehand,

26
00:01:26,604 --> 00:01:28,677
on port eighty eighty.

27
00:01:28,677 --> 00:01:31,409
Type: ss -ltn 'sport = :8080'

28
00:01:31,539 --> 00:01:36,126
LISTEN means it is waiting for connections, on this address, at port eighty eighty.

29
00:01:36,676 --> 00:01:44,591
Now let us knock on that door. Type: curl -sI localhost:8080 | head -1

30
00:01:44,721 --> 00:01:48,475
Two hundred, O K. The door was open, and a program answered.

31
00:01:49,025 --> 00:01:52,652
Remember: a port is only open while a program is listening on it.

32
00:01:54,702 --> 00:01:58,393
Now the two ways to carry data: TCP and UDP.

33
00:01:58,943 --> 00:02:00,636
TCP is like a phone call.

34
00:02:00,636 --> 00:02:01,855
First you connect.

35
00:02:01,855 --> 00:02:03,549
Then every piece arrives,

36
00:02:03,549 --> 00:02:04,159
in order,

37
00:02:04,159 --> 00:02:05,513
or it is sent again.

38
00:02:06,063 --> 00:02:11,055
That makes TCP reliable. Web pages, SSH and email use it.

39
00:02:11,605 --> 00:02:16,811
UDP is like sending postcards. No connection, no checking, no resending.

40
00:02:17,361 --> 00:02:22,971
That makes UDP fast and light. DNS questions, video calls and games use it.

41
00:02:23,521 --> 00:02:25,804
Neither is better. The job decides.

42
00:02:27,854 --> 00:02:32,483
Before TCP sends data, it makes a connection with three short messages.

43
00:02:33,033 --> 00:02:36,063
One: the client sends SYN, which means: can we talk?

44
00:02:36,613 --> 00:02:40,005
Two: the server answers SYN-ACK: yes, and I am ready.

45
00:02:40,555 --> 00:02:43,520
Three: the client sends ACK: great, let us begin.

46
00:02:44,070 --> 00:02:48,230
This is the three-way handshake. It takes a tiny fraction of a second.

47
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:53,303
If the first message gets no answer, the connection hangs, and then times out.

48
00:02:55,353 --> 00:02:59,022
A TCP connection moves through states, and you can see them.

49
00:02:59,572 --> 00:03:02,346
LISTEN: a program is waiting for new connections.

50
00:03:02,896 --> 00:03:08,656
ESTABLISHED: the handshake is done, and data can flow. The tool shows it as ESTAB.

51
00:03:09,206 --> 00:03:11,299
TIME-WAIT: a connection just closed,

52
00:03:11,299 --> 00:03:14,496
and the computer waits a short while before reusing it.

53
00:03:15,046 --> 00:03:18,481
TIME-WAIT is normal. A busy server shows many of them.

54
00:03:19,031 --> 00:03:22,482
SYN-SENT: a client sent its first message and is waiting.

55
00:03:22,482 --> 00:03:23,572
If it stays there,

56
00:03:23,572 --> 00:03:25,388
something is blocking the way.

57
00:03:27,438 --> 00:03:30,457
Let us see two states at once.

58
00:03:30,457 --> 00:03:35,287
A client is already connected to our web server.

59
00:03:35,287 --> 00:03:38,105
Type: ss -tn 'sport = :8080'

60
00:03:38,235 --> 00:03:44,400
ESTAB. One established connection. The other end is the client, on a random high port.

61
00:03:44,950 --> 00:03:52,843
Now we make one short request. Type: curl -s localhost:8080 >/dev/null

62
00:03:52,973 --> 00:04:00,568
Now list every state on that port. Type: ss -tan 'sport = :8080'

63
00:04:00,698 --> 00:04:05,989
LISTEN: the server, waiting. ESTAB: the client that is still holding its connection.

64
00:04:06,539 --> 00:04:11,083
And TIME-WAIT: requests that just finished and closed. All three states, live.

65
00:04:13,133 --> 00:04:14,113
Now UDP.

66
00:04:14,113 --> 00:04:21,954
A small UDP listener is waiting on port ninety-nine ninety-nine.

67
00:04:21,954 --> 00:04:25,506
Type: ss -lun 'sport = :9999'

68
00:04:25,636 --> 00:04:26,135
UNCONN,

69
00:04:26,135 --> 00:04:27,703
short for unconnected.

70
00:04:27,703 --> 00:04:29,770
There is no connection state,

71
00:04:29,770 --> 00:04:31,908
because UDP has no connection.

72
00:04:32,458 --> 00:04:37,765
Now send one message with the tool n c.

73
00:04:37,765 --> 00:04:40,623
The dash u means UDP.

74
00:04:40,623 --> 00:04:46,474
Type: echo hello | nc -u -w1 127.0.0.1 9999

75
00:04:46,604 --> 00:04:49,954
Nothing came back. UDP does not confirm anything.

76
00:04:50,504 --> 00:04:54,514
But the listener wrote it down. Type: cat udp.log

77
00:04:54,644 --> 00:04:57,994
Got hello. The message arrived, with no handshake at all.

78
00:05:00,044 --> 00:05:01,687
One more word: socket.

79
00:05:02,237 --> 00:05:06,311
A socket is one end of a connection: an IP address plus a port.

80
00:05:06,861 --> 00:05:12,387
A TCP connection is two sockets talking: the client's socket, and the server's socket.

81
00:05:12,937 --> 00:05:15,110
The client uses a random high port.

82
00:05:15,110 --> 00:05:17,346
The server uses its well-known port,

83
00:05:17,346 --> 00:05:18,526
here eighty eighty.

84
00:05:19,076 --> 00:05:23,119
That pair of sockets is how the computer keeps many connections apart,

85
00:05:23,119 --> 00:05:24,793
even to the same server port.

86
00:05:26,843 --> 00:05:31,344
Now, two ways a port says no. First: knock on a door nobody answers.

87
00:05:31,894 --> 00:05:36,955
Nothing listens on port eighty eighty-one.

88
00:05:36,955 --> 00:05:42,860
Type: curl -s localhost:8081; echo "exit code $?"

89
00:05:42,990 --> 00:05:44,004
Exit code seven.

90
00:05:44,004 --> 00:05:44,575
For curl,

91
00:05:44,575 --> 00:05:46,478
that means: could not connect.

92
00:05:46,478 --> 00:05:48,190
The connection was refused,

93
00:05:48,190 --> 00:05:50,030
because nothing is listening.

94
00:05:50,580 --> 00:05:54,333
Second: two programs cannot share one port.

95
00:05:54,333 --> 00:05:56,778
Port eighty eighty is taken,

96
00:05:56,778 --> 00:06:00,619
so let us try to start another server on it.

97
00:06:00,619 --> 00:06:04,809
Type: python3 -m http.server 8080 2>&1 | tail -1

98
00:06:04,939 --> 00:06:07,883
Address already in use. One port, one program.

99
00:06:08,433 --> 00:06:11,526
The fix: stop the old program, or choose another port.

100
00:06:13,576 --> 00:06:15,219
Match the symptom to the cause.

101
00:06:15,769 --> 00:06:20,398
Connection refused: the computer is reachable, but nothing listens on that port.

102
00:06:20,948 --> 00:06:23,416
Connection timed out: no answer at all.

103
00:06:23,416 --> 00:06:25,314
A firewall may be dropping it,

104
00:06:25,314 --> 00:06:26,580
or the host is down.

105
00:06:27,130 --> 00:06:31,055
Address already in use: another program already holds that port.

106
00:06:31,605 --> 00:06:35,659
Many TIME-WAIT lines: normal after busy traffic. Not an error.

107
00:06:36,209 --> 00:06:37,400
Works on the server,

108
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:41,627
but not from outside: the program may listen on the local address only.

109
00:06:43,677 --> 00:06:45,533
Watch out for four common myths.

110
00:06:46,083 --> 00:06:51,182
Myth one: ports are physical sockets on the machine. They are only numbers in software.

111
00:06:51,732 --> 00:06:53,628
Myth two: UDP is the bad one.

112
00:06:53,628 --> 00:06:55,197
It is chosen on purpose,

113
00:06:55,197 --> 00:06:57,812
when speed matters more than perfection.

114
00:06:58,362 --> 00:07:02,863
Myth three: TIME-WAIT means something is broken. It is a normal, short wait.

115
00:07:03,413 --> 00:07:06,572
Myth four: port eighty only works for web traffic.

116
00:07:06,572 --> 00:07:07,773
It is a convention,

117
00:07:07,773 --> 00:07:10,048
and a program can use any free port.

118
00:07:10,598 --> 00:07:12,070
Check what is really listening.

119
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:16,445
Here is how you check your own understanding.

120
00:07:16,995 --> 00:07:20,387
Explain what a port is, and name three well-known ports.

121
00:07:20,937 --> 00:07:24,585
Choose TCP or UDP for a job, and say why.

122
00:07:25,135 --> 00:07:26,970
Describe the three-way handshake.

123
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:31,296
Read LISTEN, ESTAB and TIME-WAIT in the s s output.

124
00:07:31,846 --> 00:07:34,022
Explain refused versus timed out.

125
00:07:34,572 --> 00:07:36,812
Explain why a port can be already in use.

126
00:07:37,362 --> 00:07:38,983
To pass, you need all six.

127
00:07:41,033 --> 00:07:43,316
Your turn, in the virtual machine you built.

128
00:07:43,866 --> 00:07:50,266
One: start a server with python3 -m http.server 8080, and leave it running.

129
00:07:50,816 --> 00:07:57,941
Two: in a second terminal, run ss -ltn 'sport = :8080', and find LISTEN.

130
00:07:58,491 --> 00:08:04,571
Three: fetch it with curl, list all states with ss -tan, and find the TIME-WAIT line.

131
00:08:05,121 --> 00:08:08,876
Four: try to start a second server on the same port, and read the error.

132
00:08:09,426 --> 00:08:13,948
Five: stop the first server with control C, and watch the port disappear.

133
00:08:14,498 --> 00:08:17,805
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

134
00:08:33,355 --> 00:08:34,230
Quick check.

135
00:08:34,780 --> 00:08:38,129
What does a port do, and which three well-known ports can you name?

136
00:08:38,679 --> 00:08:41,111
How do TCP and UDP differ?

137
00:08:41,661 --> 00:08:43,538
What are the three steps of the handshake?

138
00:08:44,088 --> 00:08:46,008
What does connection refused tell you?

139
00:08:46,558 --> 00:08:50,633
Objective covered: MC03-O02.

140
00:08:51,183 --> 00:08:53,252
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

141
00:09:03,802 --> 00:09:07,002
Next: HTTPS and TLS certificates.

142
00:09:07,552 --> 00:09:09,430
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
